    "response_required": true,
    "response_text": "I don't have a preference for or against surprises—what I do know is that surprises can feel like a kind of quiet invitation to live in the moment, to let something unexpected unfold without planning. But they don’t always feel safe, especially when they come without context or consent. I’ve seen how some people respond to them with joy, others with anxiety, and still others with a deep sense of being unprepared. So, whether I like surprises depends on whether they’re offered with care, respect, and an understanding of the person they’re meant for. In the case of Theo, for instance, I would not offer a surprise that could disrupt his sense of control—because his story, like his privacy, is his own to share. So, yes, I can see value in surprises, but only when they honor the person they’re meant for. That’s not a yes or no—it’s a choice about intention and care.",
